UPDATE: Because of the microtransaction issues and consequent blame game between Bethesda and Steam, I have abandoned the Steam version a while ago and been playing it on the Bethesda Launcher instead. The main advantage, other than no cash shop issues, is the fact that Bethesda does a sale on cash shop items regularly.
Now the review:
Basically, you:
* build your underground dwelling, room by room;
* assign each of your vault dwellers to rooms where they would perform best, for example: power rooms require Strenght, so you must assign to that room dwellers with high Strenght. Note: this becomes irrelevant once you max out their S.P.E.C.I.A.L. entirely;
* assemble teams to send them on quests;
* send them out alone into the Wasteland to collect caps, materials and bump into buildings to perform mini-quests.
and... that's about it, really.
The above sounds like an endless chain of repetitive, meaningless tasks. Which is true at the beggining, but things get better once you level up your dwellers to 50 and max out their specs. Mind you, that is no easu task and will take a good few months to accomplish, unless you resort to cheating, which defeats the purpose of even playing the game in the first place. Why cheat?
Now back to the game itself, everything requires your attention: assigning each dwellers to rooms, leveling them up, collecting resources they generate (unless you assign a Mr. Handy to each room, and set them to collect resources automatically), sending some of them out for exploring and gathering resources, making sure they're happy and healthy and even having them to "get it on" so to produce more vault dwellers...
You also have to fight off the odd raider/creature attack (or fire incident), which will require a lot of drag-and-dropping of armed dwellers to the affected rooms, especially at the beggining when you don't have many weapons and dwellers to spare. Funny thing is that they forgot to add Super Mutants and Bots...
Quest travelling, wasteland wandering and resource production (except collection) are allowed to progress even when you're offline. As of now, no offline attacks occur.
I've been enjoying it a lot since I started playing it last July, but I think it still needs a lot of improvements. a lot of tasks could be simplified, for example:
* heal a whole room rather than click on each dweller to heal them individually;
* sell multiple items at once rather than selling each item one by one (what if you have 1000s of items?);
* option for one pet benefiting the whole room instead of having 3 pets only adding individually to 3 dwellers only;
* better mouse controls: left click for selecting, right click for dragging, so to prevent accidental drag and drop;
* better camera control and options to enable/disable certain things, for example: camera keeps centering on dwellers you click on, which is very annoying when you want to drag them other rooms;
* select multiple dwellers/whole room to have them performing the same task, for example: Storage room is being attacked but it's empty, so select all dwellers from next door and send them to counter attack. As is it now, you have to click-drag-click-drag-click-drag-click-drag...
The above improvements wouldn't take much time to do and would vastly improve this game and tempt more people into spending cash on the shop. But will Bethesda ever do that? Judging by their corner-cutting approach to game development, I don't believe so...
Still, this game scores a respectable 7/10 in my opinion.

OK, so my review on Mafia III - I’ve been waiting some time for this, and now here it is, after more than 40 h of gameplay (main game) + 20h (season pass) it's a full completion except for the extra useless fuses (collectibles).
So, what I ENJOYED about the game:
- Storyline is well crafted (boss missions and cinematics) + a bit of previous Mafia games lore;
- Graphics are very good;
- Music is pretty good (maybe there are not too many songs, but they are suited, with even some renown tracks);
- Despite what others might say, it seems to me that Lincoln (the protagonist) has personality;
- Gameplay is fine overall; shooting is alright, and the new feature of takedowns which permits a more stealthy approach is welcome;
CONS:
- about Gameplay proggresion...so, there's a pyramid system going on, so basically to conquer a neighborhood/district one must do some boring repetitive grinding...
- Very few innovative features;
- Title Menu (after splash screen) is bad, having only two options available (Start new game or Continue);
- Physics could do with a bit of improvement;
- Engine is quite unstable in terms of framerate; no crashes from what I recall;
- The whole game seems unfinished/unpolished at times and could have done with at least 6 extra months of polishing + the addition of some immersive features and details; and here one could elaborate a bit but I'll just say this: There's a whole railway across town, but no trains at all (maybe taken out because of the unstable engine anyway) and also, the game could have been better with some extra activities (to have something extra to do after finishing the game, like in GTA perhaps - flying, hanging out with friends, playing anything, from poker to golf or whatever)...
I would mostly recommend the game to the old Mafia fans, but I also suggest no matter you were a fan of the series or not, before going for the game itself, for extra details, watch some video reviews before and determine whether it's worth it or not.

